<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Avignon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Avennio</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Avignon is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a> in Provence, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Avennio of the Cavares and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Roman Gaul</a>. The popes resided there from 1309 to 1377, building the vast Palace of the Popes, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> bought the city in 1348; it remained papal territory until France annexed it in 1791. Its historic centre is a World Heritage Site.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/avignon.jpg" alt="Avignon" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: OT Avignon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vue_a%C3%A9rienne_2_JP_Campomar.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Avignon lies on the left bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhone">Rhône</a>, a few kilometres above its confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/durance">Durance</a>, about 580 km south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, 229 km south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyon">Lyon</a> and 85 km north-north-west of Marseille. The river passes the western edge of the city in two branches, the Petit Rhône and the Grand Rhône, separated by the Île de la Barthelasse. The region is rich in limestone, and the present city walls, 4,330 metres long, were built of the soft limestone called mollasse burdigalienne. The climate is mediterranean, though sheltered inland, and the city is often windy, the strongest wind being the mistral. A medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> proverb called it windy Avignon, pest-ridden when there is no wind and wind-pestered when there is.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest forms of the name were reported by Greek writers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> and Ptolemy. The Roman name Avennio Cavarum, in Mela and Pliny, shows that Avignon was one of the three cities of the Celtic-Ligurian tribe of the Cavares, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavaillon">Cavaillon</a> and Orange. Between 1309 and 1377, during the Avignon Papacy, seven successive popes resided in the city. In 1348 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vi">Pope Clement VI</a> bought the town from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-i-of-naples">Joanna I of Naples</a>. Papal control persisted until 1791, when it became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>, which is why Avignon is also known as La Cité des Papes. Pope Gregory XI, born around 1329 and dead in 1378, was the seventh and last of the Avignon popes.</p><p>The medieval University of Avignon grew out of the schools already in the city and was formally constituted in 1303 by Boniface VIII in a papal bull. Boniface VIII and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-ii-of-naples">Charles II of Naples</a> were its first great protectors and benefactors. Its law <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, covering both civil and ecclesiastical law, was the most important throughout the university's existence. Avignon absorbed Montfavet between 1790 and 1794, ceded Morières-lès-Avignon in 1870 and ceded Le Pontet in 1925.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Avignon is the prefecture of the Vaucluse department in the region of Provence-Alpes-Côte d'Azur, with 92,188 inhabitants in 2023. About 16,000 live in the ancient centre enclosed by the medieval walls, one of the few in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> to have preserved its mediaeval city walls. The centre includes the Palais des Papes, Avignon Cathedral and the Pont d'Avignon, and was listed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> as a World Heritage Site in 1995. The Festival d'Avignon, founded in 1947, brings <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, dance, music and cinema to the town's historic monuments each summer. The song Sur le Pont d'Avignon dates from the mid-19th century, when Adolphe Adam included it in an opéra comique first performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1853.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Avignon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great St Bernard Pass and other Alpine crossings linked it with Italy, the Rhône carried traffic between the Mediterranean and the north, and cities such as Lyon, Vienne, Arles, <b>Avignon</b>, Geneva and, above all, Marseille grew as commercial centres from the eleventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-arles">Kingdom of Arles · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Robert the Wise (r. 1309–1343), praised as a learned 'new Solomon', ruled through an able bureaucracy and close ties with the papacy at <b>Avignon</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>During the residence of the popes at <b>Avignon</b>, Cardinal Albornoz restored order and issued the Egidian Constitutions in 1357, which remained the basic law of the state until 1816.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Born at Arezzo in 1304 and brought up near the papal court at <b>Avignon</b>, he gave up the study of law for letters.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>Film versions date back to 1920; Peter Brook's nine-hour play premiered in <b>Avignon</b> in 1985, and B. R. Chopra's television series Mahabharat was shown on Doordarshan in 1988.</blockquote><a href="/work/mahabharata">Mahabharata · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Guy Guenoun, son of the Jewish founder of &quot;Mon Bar&quot; in <b>Avignon</b>, the songwriter and poet André Montagard wrote the words at a table there in 1941 to enter a competition the Vichy regime had organised to give Pétain an official song; Guenoun recalled that Montagard,…</blockquote><a href="/songs/marechal-nous-voila">Maréchal, nous voilà !</a></li></ul></section>
